00:04:44.824 --> 00:04:46.240
MATTEO CHINAZZI:
Hello, Everybody.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.865
Thank you, Karan,
for the introduction,

00:04:47.865 --> 00:04:49.570
and it's great to be here.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:52.560
So I would like to start my
presentation off by asking you

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.450
one simple question.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.550
What will you do if
somebody came to you

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.910
and asked you to
model mosquito bites?

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:03.042
Well, that was our
task back in 2016.

00:05:03.042 --> 00:05:04.500
And in this
presentation, I'm going

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.070
to tell you how we developed
a model with the help of GCP

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:12.200
to study the diffusion of
Zika virus across the globe.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.550
And just to give
you some context--

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:18.950
so back in 2015, Brazil had
notified the World Health

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.080
Organization that a
new mysterious virus

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.160
was circulating in the country.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.890
And a few months
later, that virus

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:27.080
was recognized to be Zika.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.100
And moreover, during
the same year,

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.010
they also noticed
an unusual increase

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:34.440
in the number of microcephaly
cases in the country.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.850
And for the ones of you that are
not familiar with microcephaly,

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.370
it is a rare
neurological condition

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:44.390
in which kids-- infants heads
are significantly smaller

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:48.470
than they once of other
kids of the same age of sex.

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:52.340
It may cause not only physical
but also learning disabilities

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:54.410
as newborns grow.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.330
So because of this,
there was soon

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:59.570
suspected that a link exists
between the appearance of Zika

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:03.740
virus in Brazil and microcephaly
and other congenital

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.600
neurological conditions.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.710
And for this reason, the WHO
declared in February 2016

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:12.860
a public health emergency
of international concern.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:15.200
And that's actually
why we decided to start

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:17.480
working into this project.

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:19.630
So our goal was
essentially to try

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:22.630
to develop a model that
could help public health

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:25.450
officials to better understand
the evolution and the dynamic

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:28.289
of the Zika outbreak
across the world.

00:06:28.289 --> 00:06:29.830
And by the time we
were done not only

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:32.079
we have done just that, but
actually we have developed

00:06:32.079 --> 00:06:34.840
a new platform, a new framework
that would allow us to study

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.390
also other diseases as
I will tell you later

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:40.690
on that year of
this presentation.

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:42.804
So now let me tell
you how we did it.

00:06:42.804 --> 00:06:44.220
In the next few
minutes, I'm going

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:46.056
to talk to you
about three things.

00:06:46.056 --> 00:06:47.430
First, I'm going
to tell you what

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.560
were the scientific and
computational challenges

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.300
that we faced and
how we overcame them.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.870
Then I'm going to tell you what
are the main lessons to learn

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.905
well using GCP in our journey.

00:06:56.905 --> 00:06:58.530
And at the end, I
will give you a taste

00:06:58.530 --> 00:06:59.910
of the kind of
scientific results

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:03.500
that we can obtain
using our model.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:05.550
But before diving into
the scientific challenges,

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:09.320
let me take a step back and
give you a piece of good news.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:12.290
So luckily for us, at
our labs and thanks also

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.060
to our network of
collaborators, we

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.010
have access to a
team of experts that

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.770
know how to model
contagious outbreaks

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:20.830
and in particularly
at MOBS lab, we

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:22.880
have a long expediency
modeling anything

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.670
from the seasonal flu to Ebola.

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:27.590
And actually one of the
things that we do best

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:30.680
is to develop computational
and analytical models to study

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:33.350
the special spread of diseases.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.139
And at the core,
at the foundation

00:07:35.139 --> 00:07:36.680
of many of our work
projects, we have

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.860
GLEAM, which is our Global
Epidemic and Mobility

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.370
Model that essentially allows
us to simulate and track

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:45.620
the evolution of an
epidemic across the world

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.090
from beginning to end.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:52.470
So let me just spend one minute
telling you how GLEAM works.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.460
GLEAM essentially is
a piece of software

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:56.950
that allows you to
combine different data

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:00.040
sources, high-resolution
census data, mobility data

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.970
and complex disease dynamics
into a unified framework.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:05.560
And the way it works
is that it makes

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:07.660
use of this high-resolution
population data

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.900
to divide the world into
different subpopulations, which

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:14.342
are then connected to each other
through a mobility network,

00:08:14.342 --> 00:08:16.300
that for example take
into account for the fact

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:19.780
that people daily commute to
go to work or take airplanes

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:21.130
to travel.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.890
And then what the
model does is that it

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.540
takes each subpopulation
and simulated the diffusion

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.820
of a virus within that
subpopulation while

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.880
taking into account for all the
possible interconnections that

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:35.070
exist between them because
of the mobility network.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.070
So essentially what
GLEAM allows us to do

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.929
is to simulate the
evolution of an epidemic

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:40.789
at the global scale.

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:44.300
Unfortunately, though for us,
when we started this project,

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:47.690
there was one thing that
GLEAM was not able to do.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:51.220
And that was model
mosquito bites.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:52.950
So the first thing
that we had to do

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.760
was to introduce, first of
all, a mathematical model that

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:59.340
could allow us to model
mosquitoes within GLEAM.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:02.160
And second, we had to introduce
additional data sources

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.530
within the computational
framework of GLEAM

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:08.070
actually to indeed
model mosquito presence

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:09.894
in biting patterns.

00:09:09.894 --> 00:09:11.560
And the reason why
we have to do that is

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:14.500
because the main drivers of
Zika transmissions are infected

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:17.150
mosquitoes that by
biting individuals inject

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:19.120
the virus within a population.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.160
And then those same
individuals in turn,

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:24.220
when they are bitten by
otherwise healthy mosquitoes,

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:26.500
they can help propagate
dividers back to--

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:29.650
back to the mosquitoes,
and then the cycle goes on.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:34.870
However, not only we
had to, as I said,

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:36.770
add the new mathematical
model within GLEAM,

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:39.049
but we also had to add
new data sources together

00:09:39.049 --> 00:09:40.590
with the population
and mobility data

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:43.732
that we already had to
account for different things.

00:09:43.732 --> 00:09:45.440
The first one is that
we had to introduce

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.960
very detailed mosquito
maps telling us

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.790
where around the world
there are mosquitoes

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:52.910
that are able to actually
carry Zika virus.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:56.540
Then we also had to add monthly
varying temperature data

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:58.610
to actually model
mosquito life cycles--

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:00.960
so mosquito lives and deaths.

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:02.850
And then on top of
that, we also to add

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:05.160
socio-economic indicators
to account for the fact

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.310
that if you live in an
area where you have access

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.310
to better sanitation
facilities or simply there

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.890
is a more widespread
use of air conditioning,

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.350
then you are less subject
to be bitten by a mosquito

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:19.900
and therefore of
contracting Zika.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:22.150
After we have done all of
that-- however, all of this,

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:23.774
however, we are not
done, because there

00:10:23.774 --> 00:10:26.020
were other challenges
that we had to face.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:27.790
First and foremost,
we did not know

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:30.970
the [INAUDIBLE] original time
and place of introduction

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.674
of Zika virus in Brazil.

00:10:32.674 --> 00:10:34.090
And that essentially
meant that we

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:38.420
didn't know from where and when
to start our simulations from.

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:42.360
Plus, since now we have
additional complexity,

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:44.660
in our model we have many
more additional parameters

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:46.610
that we need to calibrate,
because not only we

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.590
have to trace and
simulate people movements,

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:52.220
but also mosquito presence
in biting patterns.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.230
And obviously, since
it was the first time

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:55.250
we were doing
something like this,

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.470
we also would like to have
a computational framework

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:00.410
and pipeline to allow us
to have quick iterations

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:03.980
between different
versions of our model.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:07.030
So all those problems actually
translate pretty directly

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:09.310
in some very specific
computational challenges

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:11.530
that we have to address.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:13.900
The first one is
that since we did not

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:16.960
know the original time of
the introduction of the virus

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.900
in Brazil, we had to run
longer simulations with GLEAM

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:22.940
to essentially explore back
in the past what was going on.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:25.840
And that meant that each single
simulation of a global epidemic

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:29.170
will take up to seven
minutes on average to run.

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:31.345
However, because of the
randomness that we have

00:11:31.345 --> 00:11:33.220
and the complexity that
we have in the model,

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:36.610
we actually have to run
200,000 of those simulations

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.230
to have robust results.

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:41.740
And that, unfortunately for us,
generates about 25 terabytes

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:43.400
of data that then
we have to process.

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:45.400
And as I said, we would
like to do all of this--

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:49.300
run the simulations-- and
analyze the results quickly--

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.530
meaning in less of a day.

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:54.400
And that's essentially where
GCP came in to save the day

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:57.190
and help us with our research.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.160
So what is the
cloud-based solution

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:02.610
that we devised to
address those challenges?

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:04.490
First of all, our
first stop was actually

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:07.050
to deploy GLEAM
on compute engine

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:09.050
to essentially help
us scale up in terms

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:12.194
of the number of parallel jobs
we could run at the same time.

00:12:12.194 --> 00:12:13.610
And to give you
an idea that meant

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:17.780
that the peak research hours we
were using up to 30,000 cores

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:19.650
at the time.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:23.060
Moreover, since we did not
want to actually saturate our

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:25.190
on prem NFS
partitions, we decided

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:27.380
to store from the very
beginning all our work

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:29.780
data and raw data
on cloud storage

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:32.660
and to use the power of
BigQuery to actually parse

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:34.495
all those terabytes
of data that we

00:12:34.495 --> 00:12:37.550
were generating to have
essentially all our simulations

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:40.070
done and analyzed within a day.

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:42.680
And then our pipeline we
close essentially moving back

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:45.214
all the process data
to our local machines,

00:12:45.214 --> 00:12:47.630
where we will generate then
the figures and the numbers we

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:49.580
want it to look
at, to analyze what

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:52.560
happened with our simulations.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.990
But let me give you some
numbers to get an understanding

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:57.240
of the scale of our efforts.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.700
So overall, we simulated
more than 10 million

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:02.130
global epidemics from
beginning to end.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:04.280
And that amounted to
about-- to more actually

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:06.540
than 250 compute years.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.400
And to run all the
simulations, we

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:12.780
deployed more than 450,000
instances in the compute engine

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:17.790
and analyzed more than 300
terabytes of data on BigQuery.

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:21.390
While doing this, we
learned a few things.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:24.650
First off, we learned that in
order to save money actually

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.140
was better to move
from standard VM

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:29.360
to preemptibles,
because that essentially

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:31.520
allowed us to run five
simulations for the price

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.970
of one and to provide GLEAM--
so to provide our software,

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.110
for example, in
terms of memory usage

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.290
to then allow us to use
custom machines instead

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:42.100
of regular instances.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:43.810
And in term of
time-saving, what we

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:46.300
found was that it was extremely
beneficial to actually

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:49.600
run many concurrent job,
loading job, in BigQuery

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:51.670
to drastically reduce
the amount of time

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.260
we had to wait to
actually populate

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.600
the database with all
the terabytes of data

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:57.100
that we were generating.

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:00.640
And last, but not least, we
automatized our entire pipeline

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:03.310
using the GCP client
library for Python

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:07.000
to allow us essentially to run
all the simulations plus all

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:08.620
the analyzes and
get to the very end,

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:11.330
the very nice PDF report with
all the numbers and figures

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:13.480
we wanted to look at without--

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:16.832
without human intervention.

00:14:16.832 --> 00:14:19.040
So now let me give you a
taste of the kind of results

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:21.770
and the kind of things
we can do with this.

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:25.390
So first of all, we can use
GLEAM as our own personal time

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:28.030
machine to go back in the
past and try to figure out

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:30.910
what was the original time
and place of introduction

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:32.302
of dividers in Brazil.

00:14:32.302 --> 00:14:33.760
And for example,
what you see there

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.520
is that we find that the most
probable time of introduction

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:40.809
was between September
2013 and February 2014.

00:14:40.809 --> 00:14:42.850
But of course, what we
can do is that we can also

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:44.254
peek into the future.

00:14:44.254 --> 00:14:45.670
So what we can do,
for example, is

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:48.730
to generate the very detailed
maps for a specific time

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:51.130
period telling us how
many Zika infections there

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:54.010
were in specific
places in the world.

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:55.810
And we can also,
since we are tracking

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:57.940
traveling within
our simulations,

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:00.010
account for how many
new cases of Zika

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:01.900
are imported because
of our traveling

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:04.870
to different countries and
then compare those data

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.540
with the ones that are
actually observed and collected

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:12.490
by local authorities, like for
example the CDC in this case.

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:14.500
So what's next for us?

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:17.830
Well, as I told you at the
beginning, our platform now

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:20.050
of course model Zika
virus, but in principle can

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:21.790
be used and extended
to study also

00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:25.570
other vector-borne diseases, for
example, dengue, yellow fever,

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:27.009
and chikungunya.

00:15:27.009 --> 00:15:29.050
Therefore, please stay
tuned to hear more from us

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:30.610
in the future regarding this.

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:32.320
But if in the meantime
you want to know

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:34.180
more about all our
other projects and all

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:37.444
the amazing people that
actually worked on this project,

00:15:37.444 --> 00:15:38.860
please have a look
at our websites

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:41.110
where you can find more
information about this project

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:43.790
and all the other amazing
things we have been doing.

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:45.660
And thank you for
your attention.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:47.330
I want to leave the
floor to Salvador

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:51.430
to tell you about how you can
stimulate the brain with GCP.

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:52.294
Thank you.

00:15:52.294 --> 00:15:55.752
[CLAPPING]

00:16:04.644 --> 00:16:07.440
SALVADOR DURA-BERNAL:
Hi, everyone.

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:10.610
So I want to start by
quoting President Obama.

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:14.640
Two years ago, he was
surprised that as human beings

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:18.960
we were able to understand
particle, subatomic particles.

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:22.320
We were able to understand
galaxies light years away,

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.510
but we were still
unable to understand

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:26.610
the brain, the
mysteries of the brain,

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:28.840
that three pounds of
matter between our ears,

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:31.080
which is basically
responsible for everything

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:35.000
that we experience and
everything that we do.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:37.620
He knew this knowledge
could be transformative.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:41.960
So he decided to start a massive
free research project called

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:44.330
the brain initiative,
which is over $4 billion,

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:47.370
to try to understand the brain.

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:51.050
Many other countries in the
world have done similar things.

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:53.150
And that's why
understanding the brain

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:56.080
is now considered
the new space race.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:58.940
So today, I want to
talk about three things.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:01.860
First, why study the
brain, why is it important,

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:05.040
how we can use simulations
to understand the brain,

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:09.790
and how will cloud help
to run these simulations.

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:12.780
So why study the brain?

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:14.550
The main motivation
for me was knowing

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:18.310
that one out of six people
suffers a brain disorder.

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:23.369
These range from depression,
anxiety, Parkinson's, epilepsy,

00:17:23.369 --> 00:17:25.720
schizophrenia.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:28.860
So this means that if you
count five people in you row,

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:31.530
one of you is likely to
have one of these disorders.

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:36.370
As an example, I
want to show you

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:38.870
what we can do to help
with these disorders.

00:17:38.870 --> 00:17:44.000
So I'm going to show you a video
from "CBS 60 Minutes" magazine,

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:46.490
which shows a woman who's
paralyzed from the neck

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:47.570
downwards.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:49.760
So she cannot move
any part of her body,

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:53.090
but she has an electrode
implanted in her brain.

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:55.550
So with these
electrodes, we can read

00:17:55.550 --> 00:17:58.790
the signals of the motor cortex,
which controls the movements.

00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:02.420
And then she is able
to move an arm almost

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:04.072
as if she wasn't paralyzed.

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:15.730
There's no sound,
but she's basically

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:18.160
explained that she can
move the arm up and down.

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:21.790
She can close the
fist, open the fits,

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:24.610
and she can now
even try to shake

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:26.294
the hand of the presenter.

00:18:37.864 --> 00:18:40.030
The presenter is pretty
amazed that this can happen.

00:18:40.030 --> 00:18:41.580
And now she offers
to do a fist bump.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.417
This is obviously
pretty amazing.

00:18:51.417 --> 00:18:53.500
And the reason this can
happen is that we actually

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:55.720
understand how some of
the signals in the brain

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:56.710
are encoded.

00:18:56.710 --> 00:18:59.540
And we can use that
information to decode them.

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:01.120
But of course, there
are many things

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:03.610
that we can do now
to improve these.

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:05.380
As you saw, the
movements are not yet

00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:07.619
very fluid, very smooth.

00:19:07.619 --> 00:19:09.160
So there's many
things that we can do

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:11.700
to understand the brain better.

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:14.610
So how do simulations help
understanding the brain?

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:17.040
First, I want to start
explaining why the brain is

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:18.570
so complex to understand.

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:20.910
And the reason is that
there are many levels, which

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:22.350
are nested inside each other.

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:26.430
So this could be like an
analogy to nested Russian dolls.

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:28.620
So if you start from
the higher level,

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:30.330
you see that these
outer layer called

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:33.390
the cortex is sort of if
you stretch it out it would

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:35.610
be like the size of a pizza.

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:37.710
And it has many
different regions,

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:39.930
which have different
functions-- so for example,

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:44.500
to process vision,
to move your body.

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:47.400
So if you go and zoom into one
of these regions, what you see

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:50.505
is a very complex
circuit of neurons.

00:19:50.505 --> 00:19:52.380
They are also structured
in different layers.

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:54.030
There's six layers of neurons.

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:56.150
And they process
all the information,

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:58.660
encode what you're doing.

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:02.040
And if you go even and zoom into
one of these single neurons,

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:05.190
you will see that this is
also a very complex machine.

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:08.430
So it has lots of chemical
processes going on,

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:11.010
lots of electrical activity.

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:13.110
And what's interesting
and complicated

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:15.420
is that these three
different levels

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:16.750
interact with each other.

00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:20.070
So if you want to test something
at the molecular level--

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:23.250
so you, for example, try
an antidepressant drug,

00:20:23.250 --> 00:20:25.980
an anti-epileptic
drug, caffeine--

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:28.230
you have to check what
happens at the single cell,

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:29.520
single neuron level.

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:32.280
That's going to affect the
circuit level, then in turn

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:35.260
the whole brain
oscillations, for example,

00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:39.750
and finally, the behavior
of the human being.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:43.570
So how can we make sense
of all this complexity?

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:46.410
So luckily for us, there's
been many years now

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:49.260
of experiments done
on the brain, both

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:52.230
measuring the shape of
neurons, how they're connected,

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:54.240
the function of the neurons.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:55.890
And what we can
do with simulation

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:59.710
is try to put all
these together.

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:01.380
So here is an example
of a simulation

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:05.210
based on lots of experimental
data from many years.

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:08.480
And what we do is reproduce
as accurately as possible

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:10.670
the experimental
data that we have.

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:12.910
And then we can use
these models, which

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:15.550
are very similar
to the real brain,

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:18.700
to test experiment with,
for example, new treatments

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:21.900
to treat some of these
disorders that we saw.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:24.440
The advantage of
having a simulation

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:28.680
is basically that you can
test as many combinations

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:29.310
as you need.

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:33.970
So in the real
brain it's very hard

00:21:33.970 --> 00:21:37.310
to test different treatments,
because of ethics reasons.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:40.120
So in the simulation, you can
do thousands of different tests

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:43.070
with different parameters,
different combinations.

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:45.580
So I'm going to show you an
example of some of the modeling

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:46.577
that I do.

00:21:46.577 --> 00:21:48.910
It's centered in the motor
cortex, the part of the brain

00:21:48.910 --> 00:21:51.070
that control most movements.

00:21:51.070 --> 00:21:52.880
And here I'm showing
the basic level.

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:56.440
So this is a single neuron
from the motor cortex.

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:59.110
And it's important to model very
accurately the single neurons,

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:02.510
because they are basically the
building blocks of the brain.

00:22:02.510 --> 00:22:05.040
So a single neuron can be
responsible for our perception.

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:08.470
It can be responsible
for moving an arm.

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:10.870
They integrate information
from lots of other neurons

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:14.320
and then perform functions.

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:15.950
The shape is an
important aspect.

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:17.741
So if you have the
wrong shape in a neuron,

00:22:17.741 --> 00:22:22.000
for example, less branches,
that could lead to disorders

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:25.630
like Alzheimer's or if you have
a neuron that responds too much

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:28.040
electrically that
could lead to epilepsy.

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:30.730
So it's important to model
very accurately both the shape

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:32.209
as we see here.

00:22:32.209 --> 00:22:34.000
On the left, we have
the experimental data.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:36.650
That's an image
from 3D microscopy

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:39.340
and then the simulation
would just be very similar

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:40.990
and also the
electrical response.

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:42.780
So we have the
experimental data.

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:45.230
And we have this
simulation, which is, again,

00:22:45.230 --> 00:22:49.100
very similar to the original.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:51.540
So to simulate these
neurons, we use

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:55.300
a very detailed mathematical
model, which implies--

00:22:55.300 --> 00:22:58.720
requires solving
differential equations,

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:01.810
hundreds of differential
equations every millisecond.

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:05.552
And it basically calculates
the currents and the voltages

00:23:05.552 --> 00:23:06.760
at every point of the neuron.

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:12.780
So this was an example for a
single neuron, but what I do

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:14.580
is simulate a circuit.

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:17.005
So I simulate a
tissues, a brain tissue

00:23:17.005 --> 00:23:20.050
of around the size
of a grain of salt,

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:22.490
which includes 10,000 neurons--

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:24.560
as detailed as the
one I showed before--

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:27.120
and over 30 million connections.

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:30.090
So these connections are
based on experimental data.

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:32.360
They depend on the
cell type on the layers

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:33.570
where they're positioned.

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:35.610
They have inputs
from other regions.

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:37.470
And using this model,
we can now check

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:40.800
how information is processed,
encoded in these circuits.

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:44.115
And we can also test all these
different drugs and treatments.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:51.470
So that's an example of how much
computing power this requires.

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:54.950
One second of the simulation
with 10,000 neurons and 30

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:59.420
million connections requires
around one hour in 50 cores

00:23:59.420 --> 00:24:00.757
of the supercomputer.

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:07.280
So how do they use Google
Cloud to simulate the brain?

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:09.800
So I mentioned one of the
simulations required 50 cores.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:12.740
But of course, we need to run
thousands of these simulations.

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:13.610
Why?

00:24:13.610 --> 00:24:16.340
First, because we need to
optimize the model to reproduce

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:17.960
the real brain biology.

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:20.240
So we have to run many
parameter combinations

00:24:20.240 --> 00:24:22.100
to check that our
model actually that's

00:24:22.100 --> 00:24:23.870
what the real brain does.

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:26.780
And secondly, once we have
a model which is actually

00:24:26.780 --> 00:24:30.590
close to the real brain, we can
use it to experiment with it.

00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:32.810
We can, for example, there's
all these treatments--

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:35.730
test also electrical
stimulation treatments,

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:41.010
which is a new type of method
that is used to cure disease.

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:44.110
And so we need to run
thousands of these simulations.

00:24:44.110 --> 00:24:47.500
So as an example, if I wanted
to run 1,000 of these one

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:53.140
second simulations, I would
require 50,000 core hours,

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:57.230
one hour and 50,000 cores.

00:24:57.230 --> 00:24:59.210
So this is a massive
amount of computing power

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:00.150
that is required.

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:02.090
And I want to show you
the evolution of how

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:03.590
I started using supercomputers.

00:25:03.590 --> 00:25:08.130
We started with a university
cluster, which had 500 cores.

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:12.280
And this very quickly
showed it was very limited.

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:16.560
We then used an NSF
scientific shared cluster

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:19.000
with around 7,000 cores.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:24.730
But it also turned out to be
not enough for our simulations.

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:27.480
And we have very recently
moved to Google Cloud, which

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:31.190
allows us to use up to
50,000 cores at the moment,

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:33.930
but you can actually use more.

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:37.620
And this translates just
as an example to show you

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:40.530
the amount of time required
to run these 1,000 brain

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:41.550
simulations.

00:25:41.550 --> 00:25:43.660
So in the original, you
never see the cluster.

00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:47.026
That would be over 120 hours.

00:25:47.026 --> 00:25:48.400
In the scientific
shared cluster,

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:50.350
it would be over 30 hours.

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:53.170
And in Google Cloud, I
can just use one hour

00:25:53.170 --> 00:25:54.820
to run all these simulations.

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:56.740
So this for me means
that I can speed up

00:25:56.740 --> 00:25:58.690
my research enormously.

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:00.880
I can think of an
idea, a treatment,

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:02.530
design the simulation,
and test it

00:26:02.530 --> 00:26:04.790
in just one hour, which
is really helpful for me.

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:12.310
So I just want to show you some
of the results illustrating

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:15.010
some of the results
obtaining these simulations.

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:17.440
So here we see in the
background our representation

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:19.960
of the motor cortex circuits.

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:24.920
And we see on the top-left,
the diffusion of calcium,

00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:28.640
which is one of the molecules
used in synaptic plasticity.

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:31.180
So it's involved in learning,
in Alzheimer's, and we

00:26:31.180 --> 00:26:33.350
can see how it diffuses
around the neuron.

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:35.545
We can also see the
electrical activity,

00:26:35.545 --> 00:26:38.860
the spikes of the neuron, at the
bottom, which is the way that

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:40.660
neurons communicate.

00:26:40.660 --> 00:26:44.440
And we can see two measures
of the statistics of how

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:47.380
populations fire
and the oscillations

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:48.340
of these populations.

00:26:48.340 --> 00:26:51.251
These are related, for example,
to things like epilepsy.

00:26:56.644 --> 00:26:58.060
So what are some
of the advantages

00:26:58.060 --> 00:27:00.910
of using Google Cloud, in
particular compute engines,

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:02.600
which is what I used?

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:06.070
As I mentioned already, I have
virtually unlimited number

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:08.360
of nodes that I can use.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:09.470
And I have no queues.

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:11.300
In most of the other
supercomputers,

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:13.760
there are queues of
over one or two days

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:16.040
before the jobs
actually start running.

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:21.650
The other advantages I can
deploy fully customized

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:22.150
clusters.

00:27:22.150 --> 00:27:25.990
So I can build the cluster to
my requirements specifically,

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:29.260
which makes it very efficient.

00:27:29.260 --> 00:27:32.090
And the cluster importantly
scales up automatically.

00:27:32.090 --> 00:27:34.810
So as soon as I start
submitting jobs,

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:36.970
new nodes start lighting up.

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:39.670
And once they are-- the jobs
finished, they turn off.

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:42.418
So that makes it very efficient
and very cost-effective.

00:27:45.410 --> 00:27:47.930
It is also integrated
with this alarm workload

00:27:47.930 --> 00:27:51.280
manager, which allows you
to submit jobs very easily.

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:54.260
It allows to have several users
connect to the supercomputer

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:55.910
and submit jobs.

00:27:55.910 --> 00:27:58.760
And it allowed me to reuse
some of that code, the scripts

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:03.170
that I had for previously
tools that I use using Slurm,

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:05.960
so I could just deploy
them and use them directly

00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:06.830
in Google Cloud.

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:13.120
Finally, I'm also
using taking advantage

00:28:13.120 --> 00:28:15.280
of these preemptible
nodes, which are basically

00:28:15.280 --> 00:28:17.050
four times cheaper.

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:19.050
The disadvantage is
that they get preempted.

00:28:19.050 --> 00:28:22.850
So they are taken away
around 10% of the time.

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:25.690
So to minimize the impact
of getting preempted,

00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:27.670
we learned a couple of tricks.

00:28:27.670 --> 00:28:31.270
In particular, if you balance
the duration of this simulation

00:28:31.270 --> 00:28:34.180
and the number of cores you
use, by using less cores

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:35.650
you can minimize--

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:39.990
reduce the chance of these
nodes getting preempted.

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:42.690
You can also run during the
night, which is a simple trick,

00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:46.150
and it means that less of
the jobs get preempted.

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:49.260
And you can also generate
scripts that automatically

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:50.970
resubmit that job so
that you don't have

00:28:50.970 --> 00:28:54.360
to take into account where
some jobs finished or not

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:55.230
and they are just--

00:28:55.230 --> 00:28:57.935
after some time that
will be completed.

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:07.040
So I just want to finish by
concluding that Google Cloud is

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:10.427
basically helping us understand
and treat brain disorders.

00:29:10.427 --> 00:29:12.260
As I mentioned before,
these brain disorders

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:15.930
affect a large part
of the population.

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:19.040
And so there are many
promising lines of research now

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:20.510
that are happening.

00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:22.670
And this space race for
understanding the brain

00:29:22.670 --> 00:29:23.970
is just beginning.

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:26.810
So I think we can all look
forward to very exciting times

00:29:26.810 --> 00:29:27.440
ahead.

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:28.230
Thank you.

00:29:28.230 --> 00:29:31.170
[CLAPPING]

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:37.370
And I want to pass to Ben,
who's going to be talking

00:29:37.370 --> 00:29:38.792
about investigating microbes.

00:29:38.792 --> 00:29:39.750
BEN TORKIAN: Thank you.

00:29:44.680 --> 00:29:45.850
Hi, everyone.

00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:48.420
I want to start my
talk with this quote,

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:50.230
"microbes run the world."

00:29:50.230 --> 00:29:54.200
More than half of the
oxygen you're breathing

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:56.090
is generated by microbes.

00:29:56.090 --> 00:29:59.520
More than half of
our body is microbe.

00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:02.100
And studying the
microbial ecosystem

00:30:02.100 --> 00:30:04.590
are key to our survival.

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:09.360
One of the material to study
microbial life is metagenomics.

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:12.860
Metagenomics-- we go and take
a sample from the environment

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:16.940
and trying to understand who is
there and what they are doing.

00:30:16.940 --> 00:30:21.170
And this give us
a snapshot we can

00:30:21.170 --> 00:30:26.030
try to gather and
understand what's

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:27.950
happening in that environment.

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:30.740
And our goal is to
collect as many snapshot

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:34.430
as possible to be able to
hopefully make a climate change

00:30:34.430 --> 00:30:35.180
model.

00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:38.690
And then we see how the
microbial life changing

00:30:38.690 --> 00:30:43.550
as climate changing, because
that's going to be key for us.

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:46.760
In our study, we
actually go to the island

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:49.220
in Bahamas called San Salvador.

00:30:49.220 --> 00:30:52.340
And you can see these islands
are really unique, because they

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:54.260
have many, many ponds.

00:30:54.260 --> 00:30:57.440
And you can see the study pond--
the one we are doing our work

00:30:57.440 --> 00:30:59.660
is marked with a red
arrow right there--

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:03.090
they are unique because
they are close to the ocean,

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:04.720
but they're a closed pond.

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:06.530
They are not open to the water.

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:09.530
They go through much rapid
climate change compared

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:11.120
to any other environment.

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:15.120
That enables us to observe the
climate change much faster.

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:17.060
And when we take
a sample, we can

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:20.150
have a better understanding of
how the climate change affects

00:31:20.150 --> 00:31:21.170
our sample.

00:31:21.170 --> 00:31:24.230
And we're trying to kind of
collect all this information

00:31:24.230 --> 00:31:28.850
whenever we taking any samples
from this environments.

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:30.426
And I'm sure, same
Bahamas, that's

00:31:30.426 --> 00:31:31.550
what you guys are thinking.

00:31:31.550 --> 00:31:32.930
Yes, it's beautiful.

00:31:32.930 --> 00:31:34.490
We really have fun going there.

00:31:34.490 --> 00:31:37.010
But most times,
this is our views,

00:31:37.010 --> 00:31:40.010
because these ponds are
so close to environment,

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:42.290
they go through really
harsh environment.

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:45.560
And in this pond, you can see
the salinity is really high.

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:47.150
The water turns yellow.

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:49.430
We saw even purple
or other colors,

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:51.700
depending on what happens
with that environment,

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:53.430
the color of water changing.

00:31:53.430 --> 00:31:55.700
And that's really
interests us to see

00:31:55.700 --> 00:32:01.440
what happens to those microbial
ecosystem in that environment.

00:32:01.440 --> 00:32:04.050
And as mentioned, we go
there literally trying

00:32:04.050 --> 00:32:06.030
to not disturb the
environment and trying

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:08.610
to pick up the sample,
then take the sample

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:12.510
to our lab trying to sequence
it and run our computation

00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:16.110
to understanding what's
going on in our sample.

00:32:16.110 --> 00:32:19.830
But still, we are dealing with
two main issue, one of them

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:22.590
are data getting
bigger and bigger.

00:32:22.590 --> 00:32:25.830
I'm sure everybody here heard
the sequencing technology

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:27.350
getting cheaper and cheaper.

00:32:27.350 --> 00:32:30.150
We're sequencing our
own DNA, our dogs now.

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:32.880
And we're getting
more and more data.

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:36.420
And really in our experiments
for each snapshot,

00:32:36.420 --> 00:32:38.490
like 10 terabytes of data.

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:41.900
For this study we are working
on, we have 10 snapshot.

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:45.540
We're dealing with 100
terabytes of data to compute.

00:32:45.540 --> 00:32:47.910
And that's a lot of
data to just go through.

00:32:47.910 --> 00:32:52.810
Also, if you guys know by
Informatica [INAUDIBLE],,

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:55.030
they're computationally
expensive.

00:32:55.030 --> 00:32:58.440
Everybody I'm sure heard
about assembly or alignment.

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:01.210
There have a lot of
optimization to these algorithm,

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:04.740
but still they are heavily
computationally expensive.

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:08.310
And there are not much we can
do but just compute the data.

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:13.080
As this data growing, the
compute time growing too.

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:15.760
What we try to do,
we come back and look

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:18.000
at our workflow in a lab.

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:21.600
And we understand the compute
time and analytic part

00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:23.400
is the main part
we need to focus

00:33:23.400 --> 00:33:25.320
in trying to solve the issue.

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:29.640
And what we kind of trying
to go our university cluster

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:31.670
and see what we
can do and change

00:33:31.670 --> 00:33:36.270
our workflow to be able to
compute our data much faster.

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:38.490
We come up with
our own workflow.

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:42.610
We design our own workflow to
be able to scale up our job.

00:33:42.610 --> 00:33:45.170
But if you're familiar
with the workflows,

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:47.670
the second you see this
picture, you should recall this.

00:33:47.670 --> 00:33:49.140
This is MapReduce.

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:52.050
What we did we add up
the MapReduce model

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:53.280
to our workflow.

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:54.900
And you can see--

00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:56.620
in the top, you can
mention all the parts

00:33:56.620 --> 00:33:59.200
we are doing to our
workflow is in the top.

00:33:59.200 --> 00:34:03.382
And you can see how it is
matched to the MapReduce.

00:34:03.382 --> 00:34:04.840
And that was the
reason we thought,

00:34:04.840 --> 00:34:05.970
oh, we solved the issue.

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:08.370
Now, we can scale
as much as we need.

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:11.280
But in reality, when
we done run our job,

00:34:11.280 --> 00:34:15.120
we were really happy to see
the job was taken seven years

00:34:15.120 --> 00:34:17.460
to change to 3 and 1/2 months.

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:21.199
But if you know research, most
times you have one or two year.

00:34:21.199 --> 00:34:23.780
If you get a life
grant, it's five years.

00:34:23.780 --> 00:34:26.197
And I mentioned we
have 10 snapshot.

00:34:26.197 --> 00:34:28.530
In this case, we're going to
be around three months just

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:29.790
computing the data.

00:34:29.790 --> 00:34:32.040
I don't know when they're
going to be able to actually

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:33.810
publish or do the work.

00:34:33.810 --> 00:34:36.780
That was the reason we kind of
a reached our friend at Google

00:34:36.780 --> 00:34:38.469
and asked them for help.

00:34:38.469 --> 00:34:42.600
And we estimate, after
we transition to GCP,

00:34:42.600 --> 00:34:45.520
we can finish the
same work in 12 hours.

00:34:45.520 --> 00:34:46.679
That's a big deal.

00:34:46.679 --> 00:34:48.150
I'm getting right
here to my friend

00:34:48.150 --> 00:34:51.179
Paul to tell you guys
how we move our workflow

00:34:51.179 --> 00:34:54.261
from our local cluster to GCP.

00:34:54.261 --> 00:34:54.760
Thank you.

00:35:05.069 --> 00:35:05.860
PAUL SAGONA: Hello.

00:35:05.860 --> 00:35:06.365
All right.

00:35:06.365 --> 00:35:07.680
No, that's all right.

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:08.850
Thanks, man.

00:35:08.850 --> 00:35:11.440
Yeah, so as part of the
HPC team at the University

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:14.710
our job is to help them
accelerate their research.

00:35:14.710 --> 00:35:20.040
And if we see that we can go
from seven years to half a day,

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:22.280
well, we need to
make that happen.

00:35:22.280 --> 00:35:26.170
So our first step was
to come up with a plan.

00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:28.720
We really wanted
to deploy a cluster

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:32.140
in the cloud that happened
to look like our environment

00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:33.550
back home.

00:35:33.550 --> 00:35:36.610
We wanted to dynamically
scale that environment based

00:35:36.610 --> 00:35:40.580
on his job size or
future job sizes.

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:42.760
We also wanted to
dynamically install

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:46.780
a software stack based on the
software he needed or needed

00:35:46.780 --> 00:35:49.280
to change and so on.

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:51.340
We also had to come up
with the best storage

00:35:51.340 --> 00:35:54.010
solution, because we're
dealing with a lot of data.

00:35:54.010 --> 00:35:58.080
And one mistake
could cost us a lot.

00:35:58.080 --> 00:36:00.870
So next stage was
doing small test.

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:03.090
You know, we needed to
iterate through the process

00:36:03.090 --> 00:36:06.270
to basically optimize, make
sure that we're doing this

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:10.420
exactly right or else
again it could cost us.

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:13.660
We had to develop tools
to basically script out

00:36:13.660 --> 00:36:17.410
automated job submission
because of the large set of jobs

00:36:17.410 --> 00:36:19.840
and samples that we
had to work with.

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:23.350
After we did that, we're able
to scale up to smaller just

00:36:23.350 --> 00:36:25.120
to make sure our
scaling is working right

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:28.250
before we have to
send out that big job.

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:32.840
And then lastly, that big job,
we have to hit the go button,

00:36:32.840 --> 00:36:35.770
submit the entire
work set, monitor

00:36:35.770 --> 00:36:39.590
and verify that these results
are coming back right.

00:36:39.590 --> 00:36:42.182
So this is what our solution
ended up looking like.

00:36:42.182 --> 00:36:43.640
And you know, you
can kind of think

00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:47.790
of In this as our input,
our compute, and our output.

00:36:47.790 --> 00:36:52.250
Now, our input is our reference
database, which is actually

00:36:52.250 --> 00:36:55.490
going to be the same for
every job that we run

00:36:55.490 --> 00:36:57.600
and then of course,
our sample database.

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:01.250
We ended up using an
attached persistent SSD

00:37:01.250 --> 00:37:02.600
for this entire run.

00:37:02.600 --> 00:37:06.260
Every job had this
attached persistent SSD,

00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:09.350
but the key was
it was read only.

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:11.330
This worked extremely
well for us.

00:37:11.330 --> 00:37:14.490
It was fast and
scaled extremely well.

00:37:14.490 --> 00:37:18.000
Our compute-- so our compute
is our dynamic HPC cluster.

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:20.880
And as I said, I wanted this
to look like our cluster

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:22.290
back home.

00:37:22.290 --> 00:37:24.330
So it does.

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:26.340
We have a login node,
a controller node,

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:27.960
and our compute stack.

00:37:27.960 --> 00:37:30.960
Our compute stack ended
up being the 32 core

00:37:30.960 --> 00:37:33.300
instances out there.

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:36.280
Now, this is all managed with
a Slurm workload manager.

00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:41.110
So as mentioned in
the previous talk,

00:37:41.110 --> 00:37:46.280
our jobs actually
scaled with the cluster.

00:37:46.280 --> 00:37:48.350
So it worked out great.

00:37:48.350 --> 00:37:52.110
Our output-- so our outputs
are actually relatively small.

00:37:52.110 --> 00:37:56.210
And you know, we didn't
really need a big scale SSDs,

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:58.340
big scale file storage.

00:37:58.340 --> 00:38:01.010
We actually sent our data
at the end of our jobs

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:04.310
to a Google Cloud bucket.

00:38:04.310 --> 00:38:12.200
So the job-- we ran on a
124,352 cores concurrently.

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:16.820
We ran on 303,886 nodes.

00:38:16.820 --> 00:38:18.710
And we did that in
16 and 1/2 hours.

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:23.820
So challenges and
findings for our run,

00:38:23.820 --> 00:38:25.600
it was enormously successful.

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:27.510
I think we had two
instances where

00:38:27.510 --> 00:38:30.060
the nodes didn't come up right.

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:32.070
We resubmitted those
jobs automatically

00:38:32.070 --> 00:38:33.660
through our scheduler.

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:35.330
It worked great.

00:38:35.330 --> 00:38:37.580
During the testing
phase, as I mentioned,

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:40.160
we had to find
that best storage.

00:38:40.160 --> 00:38:41.630
We tried NFS.

00:38:41.630 --> 00:38:44.410
We tried one of the Elastifile
file-- which was neat,

00:38:44.410 --> 00:38:46.430
but kind of overkill--

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:48.182
and tried some
local disk options.

00:38:48.182 --> 00:38:50.390
And you know, we ended up
going with that combination

00:38:50.390 --> 00:38:55.250
of the local SSD and connected
to that persisted SSD.

00:38:55.250 --> 00:38:57.100
It worked great.

00:38:57.100 --> 00:38:58.720
Slurm-- so Slurm
was a lot of fun.

00:38:58.720 --> 00:39:01.876
You know, we used the term
workload manager back home

00:39:01.876 --> 00:39:02.500
on our cluster.

00:39:02.500 --> 00:39:05.770
So we were already
very familiar.

00:39:05.770 --> 00:39:08.674
Working with the Google
team and the Slurm team,

00:39:08.674 --> 00:39:10.090
we went through a
lot of tweaking,

00:39:10.090 --> 00:39:13.000
a lot of making it
just how we wanted.

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:16.630
Lastly, something we didn't
anticipate is hyper-threading.

00:39:16.630 --> 00:39:20.260
In our HPC space and
in most HPC shops,

00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:22.150
we don't use hyper-threading.

00:39:22.150 --> 00:39:24.160
It just doesn't
provide any advantage.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:26.260
It's already enabled
in the cloud.

00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:28.900
So that actually affected
our runtime slightly.

00:39:28.900 --> 00:39:32.800
So it's just something for us
to keep in mind in the future.

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:35.990
So our takeaway and
our key next steps,

00:39:35.990 --> 00:39:38.600
this greatly, greatly
enhanced our research.

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:42.710
I mean, it really made this
a reality for this lab.

00:39:42.710 --> 00:39:45.320
Our collaborative process
with the Google team

00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:48.350
and the Slurm's SchedMD
team was fantastic.

00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:49.760
You know, we provided feedback.

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:51.890
They provide suggestions.

00:39:51.890 --> 00:39:54.890
I think we actually led to
some code changes, and updates,

00:39:54.890 --> 00:39:56.960
and things like that.

00:39:56.960 --> 00:40:00.440
My goal even before this
and still moving forward

00:40:00.440 --> 00:40:05.120
is us to locally burst our
environment into cloud.

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:08.480
And this is exactly what it was.

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:12.170
So we're really looking
forward to the future.

00:40:12.170 --> 00:40:14.630
And now that this has
been-- this particular one

00:40:14.630 --> 00:40:17.780
has been a success
for this research lab,

00:40:17.780 --> 00:40:21.070
that lab is now able
to seek further funding

00:40:21.070 --> 00:40:22.910
and able to expand the research.

00:40:22.910 --> 00:40:26.270
You know, climate change
doesn't happen in a day.

00:40:26.270 --> 00:40:30.250
He needs samples for
months, years, even decades.

00:40:30.250 --> 00:40:36.330
So he already has about a year's
worth of data ready to compute.

00:40:36.330 --> 00:40:38.040
And if he's tried
to continue what

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:41.310
he was doing on his local PC,
it would take him 50 years,

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:43.590
just for a year's
worth of data--

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:46.620
on our cluster, 2.2 years--

00:40:46.620 --> 00:40:48.780
and that's if I actually
let him on my cluster

00:40:48.780 --> 00:40:52.490
for 2.2 years dedicated--

00:40:52.490 --> 00:40:54.380
GCP 3 and 1/2 days.

00:40:57.820 --> 00:41:00.040
I'd like to thank Bob Doran--

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:02.110
he's another member
or a group that

00:41:02.110 --> 00:41:04.840
made this project possible--

00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:09.490
Shawn Norman-- who is the
lead PI for the research--

00:41:09.490 --> 00:41:10.870
and of course our Google team.

00:41:10.870 --> 00:41:14.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

